Description:
In this lab, you will learn how Kubernetes Services expose your applications using different types.

While ClusterIP provides internal-only access, NodePort exposes services externally on a port of every node, and LoadBalancer integrates with cloud providers to create an external load balancer.

You will deploy a simple nginx application, scale it to 2 replicas, and expose it sequentially using ClusterIP, NodePort, and LoadBalancer Services.

You will also verify that each Service correctly maps to the underlying pods via Endpoints.

The lab helps you understand:

How Services abstract pod IPs and provide stable network access.

How different Service types affect accessibility within and outside the cluster.

How Kubernetes automatically manages Endpoints when pods scale or change.

Objective:
Deploy a sample nginx application with 2 replicas.

Expose the Deployment using ClusterIP, NodePort, and LoadBalancer Services.

Observe the differences in accessibility and Endpoints for each Service type.

Gain hands-on understanding of internal vs external exposure of Services in Kubernetes.

Tasks:
Verify the namespace svc-types-lab.

Create Deployment nginx-deploy using the nginx image.

Scale the deployment to 2 replicas.

Expose the deployment as:

ClusterIP (named nginx-clusterip)

NodePort (named nginx-nodeport)

LoadBalancer (named nginx-lb)

Verify all Services exist.

Check Endpoints for all Services to confirm connectivity to pods.

Expected Outcome:
2 nginx pods running in nginx-deploy.

3 Services:

nginx-clusterip → internal access

nginx-nodeport → external access via NodeIP:NodePort

nginx-lb → external load balancer (pending locally)

All Services show correct Endpoints pointing to the pods. -->



Check namespaces:
kubectl get namespaces

→ Confirms that svc-types-lab exists or needs to be created.

Create Deployment in the namespace:
kubectl -n svc-types-lab create deployment nginx-deploy --image=nginx

→ Creates the nginx deployment.

Scale Deployment to 2 replicas:
kubectl -n svc-types-lab scale deployment nginx-deploy --replicas=2

→ Ensures multiple pods for load balancing.

Verify pods:
kubectl -n svc-types-lab get pods

→ Confirms that 2 pods are running.

Expose ClusterIP Service:
kubectl -n svc-types-lab expose deployment nginx-deploy --port=80 --target-port=80 --name=nginx-clusterip --type=ClusterIP

→ Creates internal-only access.

Verify ClusterIP Service:
kubectl -n svc-types-lab get svc nginx-clusterip

→ Confirms ClusterIP and port mapping.

Expose NodePort Service (K8s auto-assigned port):
kubectl -n svc-types-lab expose deployment nginx-deploy --port=80 --target-port=80 --name=nginx-nodeport --type=NodePort

→ Allows external access to the service via NodeIP:NodePort.

Verify NodePort Service:
kubectl -n svc-types-lab get svc nginx-nodeport

→ Confirms NodePort assigned and reachable externally.

Expose LoadBalancer Service:
kubectl -n svc-types-lab expose deployment nginx-deploy --port=80 --target-port=80 --name=nginx-lb --type=LoadBalancer

→ Provisions external access; may stay pending in local clusters.

Verify LoadBalancer Service:
kubectl -n svc-types-lab get svc nginx-lb

→ Checks external IP (pending locally) and port mapping.

List all services:
kubectl -n svc-types-lab get svc

→ Compare ClusterIP, NodePort, LoadBalancer types and accessibility.

Check endpoints for each service:

kubectl -n svc-types-lab get endpoints
→ Confirms each service points to the 2 nginx pods.
